  "reconstructibility": 2,
  "reason": "The records repeatedly describe high-strain-rate MD tensile deformation of the closely related equiatomic CoCrFeMnNi system across temperature, including thermally facilitated dislocation nucleation, reduced yielding stress at high temperature, partial-dislocation activity, stacking-fault accumulation, and local HCP identification. They therefore make a temperature- and rate-dependent tensile MD study of a related FCC FeNiCrMn single crystal, interpreted through Shockley partials and stacking faults, plausibly reconstructible. Compression, friction, creep, and refractory-alloy records add general support for thermal softening and dislocation kinetics. Important details remain unanticipated—notably vacancy-cluster nucleation sites, the particular crystallographic-direction comparison, negligible yield-rate sensitivity, and the distinction between merely HCP-like faulted regions and a true FCC-to-HCP transformation—but these are refinements rather than a substantial new intervention concept."
